1
00:00:00,880 --> 00:00:04,960
They make our lives more comfortable, more rewarding, and more secure.

2
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:11,280
They are the magical machines that have brought us to the edge of a new frontier of limitless possibilities.

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,240
But it is a frontier filled with dangers and demons of our own creation.

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:24,640
In 1979, a power plant in southeast Pennsylvania revealed how close society can come to a nuclear nightmare.

5
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:29,840
Now, inviting disaster. Three Mile Island, next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
So often with these disasters, when I looked into them, the operator was left high and dry.

7
00:00:49,680 --> 00:00:54,100
With that terrible feeling of being trapped in a machine running wild.

8
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:57,440
And nothing in the manual could help them.

9
00:00:57,441 --> 00:00:59,620
There was no one available on the phone.

10
00:00:59,880 --> 00:01:01,420
The designers were not accessible.

11
00:01:02,320 --> 00:01:03,620
And time was ticking.

12
00:01:04,020 --> 00:01:09,600
This terrible feeling that this event is going to catch you.

13
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:18,140
March 28, 1979, 3.58 a.m.

14
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:22,820
As it has for eons, the Susquehanna River continues this night

15
00:01:22,821 --> 00:01:25,220
on its ancient way through southeastern Pennsylvania.

16
00:01:25,221 --> 00:01:30,220
It glides through the state capitol at Harrisburg.

17
00:01:30,221 --> 00:01:36,220
In the capitol building, the graveyard shift is cleaning the governor's office for the coming workday.

18
00:01:36,221 --> 00:01:43,220
Only a few miles further south, the Susquehanna moves past the empty shops and silent streets of Middletown,

19
00:01:43,221 --> 00:01:52,220
then on to the village of Goldsboro, sleeping now across the river from the blinking lights of four 30-story towers.

20
00:01:52,221 --> 00:01:57,720
These enormous monoliths mark the site of the Three Mile Island nuclear power plant.

21
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:06,720
Perched on a sandbar in the river, the plant swills tons of the Susquehanna's cool water every hour.

22
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:11,900
The water absorbs the enormous heat produced by 150 tons of uranium

23
00:02:11,901 --> 00:02:15,600
that lie buried in the heart of the plant's two nuclear reactors.

24
00:02:16,780 --> 00:02:21,940
In boilers seven stories high, still other pipes carrying more water absorb this heat

25
00:02:21,941 --> 00:02:25,500
and turn it into a quarter million pounds of pressurized steam a minute.

26
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:29,340
The tremendous energy now trapped in this superheated steam

27
00:02:29,341 --> 00:02:33,500
moves on to a 500-ton turbine as long as a city block.

28
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:36,880
The turbine uses the steam's energy to spin a generator

29
00:02:36,881 --> 00:02:41,500
that at last produces the final product of this very complicated process.

30
00:02:42,440 --> 00:02:44,600
Almost a billion watts of electricity.

31
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:46,920
Once it has done its job,

32
00:02:47,460 --> 00:02:50,900
the steam transfers its scalding temperature off to more water,

33
00:02:50,901 --> 00:02:57,000
which in turn finally loses its heat, trickling down the giant cooling towers.

34
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:05,300
Three men are on duty this night inside the control room of Unit 2,

35
00:03:05,800 --> 00:03:07,600
one of the plant's two generating units.

36
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:13,400
They are responsible for over a thousand gauges, lights, meters, and switches

37
00:03:13,401 --> 00:03:15,320
that monitor the health of Unit 2.

38
00:03:17,720 --> 00:03:20,080
Tonight, repairs are being carried out.

39
00:03:20,081 --> 00:03:24,500
Workers are shooting compressed air into a pipe trying to unclog a filter.

40
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:30,200
They have done this before, but this time things will be different.

41
00:03:31,660 --> 00:03:35,560
4 a.m., what Ray Bradbury called the low ebb of the soul,

42
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,660
late at night, often the time when accidents happen.

43
00:03:39,660 --> 00:03:43,640
Using compressed air to clean a filter is a routine maintenance technique.

44
00:03:43,840 --> 00:03:48,560
But tonight, a trace of water has worked its way back into the air system,

45
00:03:48,561 --> 00:03:52,120
and in an instant, the great machine senses its presence.

46
00:03:53,500 --> 00:03:56,520
Nothing at Three Mile Island will ever again be routine.

47
00:04:02,840 --> 00:04:04,680
Suddenly alarms started going off.

48
00:04:05,620 --> 00:04:06,320
Lights flash.

49
00:04:06,700 --> 00:04:10,740
One person described the control board as lighting up like a Christmas tree.

50
00:04:11,520 --> 00:04:13,780
They didn't know what happened. They couldn't figure it out.

51
00:04:13,781 --> 00:04:19,820
The plant's computers react as though the water in the air system is a dangerous invader.

52
00:04:19,821 --> 00:04:24,820
Water in the line can lower critical air pressure to valves and pumps.

53
00:04:24,821 --> 00:04:28,820
The computers fight back by instantly shutting down those pumps.

54
00:04:28,821 --> 00:04:34,820
But this means that the all-important coolant is no longer relieving the reactor of its tremendous heat.

55
00:04:34,821 --> 00:04:40,820
And that caused the control operators to face situations that they had never encountered before

56
00:04:40,821 --> 00:04:46,380
before and had never been covered in their training or in their procedures.

57
00:04:46,381 --> 00:04:50,840
With nowhere to discharge its tremendous heat, the pressure in the primary coolant system

58
00:04:50,841 --> 00:04:53,560
within the reactor begins to build.

59
00:04:53,561 --> 00:04:58,940
Sensing danger, the computer now orders cadmium rods to plunge into the reactor, shutting

60
00:04:58,941 --> 00:05:04,260
down the chain reaction that is splitting millions of atoms.

61
00:05:04,261 --> 00:05:09,140
But even with the nuclear reaction turned off, intense heat and enormous pressure continue

62
00:05:09,141 --> 00:05:10,420
to increase.

63
00:05:10,421 --> 00:05:13,380
The heat and pressure can tear the system apart.

64
00:05:13,381 --> 00:05:16,600
A pressure-operated relief valve known as a PORV

65
00:05:16,601 --> 00:05:18,700
now opens to vent the pressure.

66
00:05:18,701 --> 00:05:22,040
All of this is normal precaution built into the system.

67
00:05:22,041 --> 00:05:25,900
What isn't normal is that the valve fails to re-close.

68
00:05:25,901 --> 00:05:30,300
It will now threaten this great machine with destruction.

69
00:05:30,301 --> 00:05:33,600
Even worse, the PORV valve indicator light on the control

70
00:05:33,601 --> 00:05:36,080
console goes out, giving the operators

71
00:05:36,081 --> 00:05:38,720
the impression that the valve, having done its job,

72
00:05:38,721 --> 00:05:40,420
has now closed.

73
00:05:40,421 --> 00:05:45,360
But what the light was designed to do was simply say the control order had been sent.

74
00:05:45,361 --> 00:05:48,500
It had been sent, but not obeyed.

75
00:05:48,501 --> 00:05:53,140
Reactor coolant is what separates a safe reactor from a nuclear nightmare.

76
00:05:53,141 --> 00:05:59,600
Now that precious coolant is gushing out of the stuck valve without anyone's knowledge.

77
00:05:59,601 --> 00:06:05,140
What is happening on Three Mile Island on this March night was never adequately planned for.

78
00:06:05,141 --> 00:06:10,260
The idea for building this remarkable machine had been born 20 years before and had grown

79
00:06:10,261 --> 00:06:14,940
in a swell of enthusiasm for what was then viewed as the perfect energy source.

80
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:21,340
As far back as the 1950s and early 60s,

81
00:06:21,740 --> 00:06:25,060
Americans were told that nuclear power could produce electricity

82
00:06:25,061 --> 00:06:28,700
that was cheaper and cleaner than that made from oil or coal.

83
00:06:29,520 --> 00:06:32,820
Proponents boasted that atomic power plants could produce electricity

84
00:06:32,821 --> 00:06:36,160
so inexpensively that it would be too cheap to meter.

85
00:06:36,161 --> 00:06:40,400
nuclear power's popularity increased even more

86
00:06:40,401 --> 00:06:45,540
when world events in 1973 signaled the end of inexpensive oil.

87
00:06:48,660 --> 00:06:52,420
In October of that year, the petroleum exporting countries

88
00:06:52,421 --> 00:06:56,520
shocked the world by drastically cutting back on petroleum sales.

89
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:01,340
As they struggled to fill their gas tanks

90
00:07:01,341 --> 00:07:04,000
and worried about fuel for heat and electricity,

91
00:07:04,220 --> 00:07:06,040
most Americans agreed.

92
00:07:06,160 --> 00:07:10,200
The infant nuclear power industry needed to grow quickly.

93
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:18,700
It created a dynamic within the federal government and the nuclear industry of,

94
00:07:19,280 --> 00:07:20,860
hey, we've got to build more nuclear plants.

95
00:07:21,380 --> 00:07:25,120
These plants grew in size by leaps and bounds.

96
00:07:25,280 --> 00:07:27,080
They were subsidized by the federal government.

97
00:07:27,320 --> 00:07:30,460
It really looked like the sky was the limit.

98
00:07:31,260 --> 00:07:35,780
By 1974, there were 43 nuclear plants around the nation,

99
00:07:35,781 --> 00:07:38,240
with another 104 on the way.

100
00:07:39,180 --> 00:07:42,320
Work on the Three Mile Island plant was begun that same year.

101
00:07:43,260 --> 00:07:46,580
The managers of the new plant would be the Metropolitan Edison Company,

102
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:47,940
known as MedEd.

103
00:07:48,460 --> 00:07:52,780
The company went to great lengths to reassure citizens living near Three Mile Island

104
00:07:52,781 --> 00:07:55,840
that the new technology was perfectly safe.

105
00:07:56,700 --> 00:08:01,200
The experts said you never have to worry about an accident

106
00:08:01,201 --> 00:08:06,020
because we have backups to backups,

107
00:08:06,500 --> 00:08:08,480
and there will never be an accident.

108
00:08:08,481 --> 00:08:14,200
We were assured that an accident like TMI was, quote-unquote, non-credible.

109
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:15,560
It couldn't happen.

110
00:08:16,800 --> 00:08:21,020
But for all the promises of the past, the accident was happening,

111
00:08:21,420 --> 00:08:22,580
and happening quickly.

112
00:08:23,140 --> 00:08:26,020
Unnoticed high up on the pressurizing tank of Unit 2,

113
00:08:26,660 --> 00:08:29,700
1,000 pounds of radioactive steam and water a minute

114
00:08:29,701 --> 00:08:32,520
continued to pour out of the stuck relief valve.

115
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:36,620
So now there was less and less coolant every minute,

116
00:08:37,520 --> 00:08:43,720
and it caused the instruments to go crazy, to lie to the operators.

117
00:08:44,720 --> 00:08:47,720
There were 2,000 instruments in the control room.

118
00:08:48,480 --> 00:08:52,000
Amazingly, none show how much coolant is in the reactor.

119
00:08:52,900 --> 00:08:59,240
The status of the reactor's all-important coolant is monitored indirectly by measuring the pressure inside the reactor.

120
00:09:00,100 --> 00:09:05,500
It's similar to showing how much water is in a tea kettle by measuring the pressure of the steam coming out.

121
00:09:07,840 --> 00:09:16,000
And what all these lights and gauges now show is a level of pressure in the system that says water is gradually filling the reactor's pressure tank.

122
00:09:16,001 --> 00:09:24,460
If allowed to fill to the top, the water will finally have nowhere to expand and will burst the critical coolant system like an overinflated balloon.

123
00:09:25,420 --> 00:09:31,100
Reactor operators call the condition going solid and have been taught never to allow it to happen.

124
00:09:32,520 --> 00:09:38,960
The pressure gauges suggest the water is continuing to rise 200, 300, 350 inches.

125
00:09:39,240 --> 00:09:42,240
The manual says 400 inches is the maximum.

126
00:09:42,241 --> 00:09:46,240
That pressurizer was shown to be full of water,

127
00:09:46,241 --> 00:09:48,240
when in fact it was actually losing water.

128
00:09:48,241 --> 00:09:51,240
It was a false indication of where the level was.

129
00:09:51,241 --> 00:09:55,240
But they responded the way the procedures and training had taught them,

130
00:09:55,241 --> 00:09:57,240
was to deal with that problem.

131
00:10:01,240 --> 00:10:03,240
5 a.m.

132
00:10:03,241 --> 00:10:06,240
The control room begins to fill with people all trying

133
00:10:06,241 --> 00:10:09,240
to bring the worsening situation under control.

134
00:10:09,241 --> 00:10:13,672
control. But the system is not behaving according to the book.

135
00:10:13,673 --> 00:10:15,960
With the pressure so high, there

136
00:10:15,961 --> 00:10:19,670
should be too much, not too little water covering the core.

137
00:10:19,671 --> 00:10:21,620
Instead, the reactor is getting

138
00:10:21,621 --> 00:10:26,900
hotter and hotter. The problem is that no one knows about the coolant gushing out of

139
00:10:26,901 --> 00:10:27,601
the valve.

140
00:10:27,602 --> 00:10:32,760
They have their instrument panel, but because of the damage, they begin to feel

141
00:10:32,761 --> 00:10:36,923
they couldn't trust the readings that they were getting on the instruments.

142
00:10:36,924 --> 00:10:37,700
As the reactor

143
00:10:37,701 --> 00:10:42,280
After coolant heats up, more of the liquid is turning to steam.

144
00:10:42,281 --> 00:10:47,580
The giant pumps that move 2,000 gallons of water a minute are not designed to handle

145
00:10:47,581 --> 00:10:49,840
the steam.

146
00:10:49,841 --> 00:10:53,160
They start to violently shake and vibrate.

147
00:10:53,161 --> 00:10:57,220
If they tear themselves apart, the plant is doomed.

148
00:10:57,221 --> 00:10:59,860
An unheard of decision is made.

149
00:10:59,861 --> 00:11:02,720
The control room supervisor will gamble.

150
00:11:02,721 --> 00:11:05,140
He orders reactor pumps turned off.

151
00:11:05,141 --> 00:11:11,080
They did exactly the wrong thing, but they thought they had never been trained for this situation.

152
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:16,400
They thought it was a way to keep the reactor from, quote, going solid.

153
00:11:16,740 --> 00:11:19,940
So they were fooled. They began letting coolant out.

154
00:11:21,080 --> 00:11:25,480
The men in the control room now mistakenly think that the situation is improving.

155
00:11:26,200 --> 00:11:26,920
It is not.

156
00:11:27,620 --> 00:11:34,460
With the pumps off, the core is being uncovered, and its temperature is over 2,000 degrees and rising.

157
00:11:35,140 --> 00:11:40,440
When the core reaches 5,000 degrees, it will melt, becoming a molten mass,

158
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:45,620
a metallic lava that will burn through the 8-inch thick steel containment vessel.

159
00:11:46,500 --> 00:11:49,760
Once out of the plant, it will scorch into the earth itself.

160
00:11:50,780 --> 00:11:55,460
What happens next could become an unrivaled technological disaster.

161
00:11:56,880 --> 00:12:00,900
When it reaches the water table, it will immediately turn to steam, boiling steam.

162
00:12:00,901 --> 00:12:07,280
there will be geysers of radioactivity steam shooting up in parking lots and driveways and

163
00:12:07,281 --> 00:12:10,368
streets and houses for miles around.

164
00:12:10,369 --> 00:12:15,000
The nightmare scenario is known as the China syndrome.

165
00:12:16,260 --> 00:12:20,900
Land surrounding the plant will become uninhabitable. A study some years earlier

166
00:12:20,901 --> 00:12:26,980
has suggested upwards of 40,000 people could die if the China syndrome becomes reality.

167
00:12:26,981 --> 00:12:32,520
that event was underway and the clock was now ticking.

168
00:12:34,380 --> 00:12:38,880
In December 1951, an experimental reactor in Idaho

169
00:12:38,881 --> 00:12:42,020
generated the first electric power from atoms,

170
00:12:42,400 --> 00:12:44,040
enough to light four bulbs.

171
00:12:45,180 --> 00:12:48,140
Inviting Disaster will continue in a moment on the History Channel.

172
00:12:49,600 --> 00:12:53,820
We now return to Inviting Disaster on Modern Marvels.

173
00:12:53,821 --> 00:12:59,220
The worst thing that can happen with a high-power complex machine is to have

174
00:12:59,221 --> 00:13:05,020
things start to go wrong and to not know as an operator what's happening or what

175
00:13:05,021 --> 00:13:07,820
you're supposed to do.

176
00:13:08,920 --> 00:13:17,380
March 28th, 1979 715 a.m. In a dozen communities surrounding the Three Mile

177
00:13:17,381 --> 00:13:21,700
Island plant people are beginning their day

178
00:13:23,320 --> 00:13:29,880
commuters start the drive to Harrisburg shop owners prepare to open children

179
00:13:29,881 --> 00:13:35,340
begin heading for school all are still unaware of the potential disaster that

180
00:13:35,341 --> 00:13:42,340
is building only a few miles away at the Three Mile Island plant a relief worker

181
00:13:42,341 --> 00:13:47,240
has finally discovered the stuck valve too late the valve has been open for

182
00:13:47,241 --> 00:13:52,160
over two hours and a quarter of a million pounds of coolant have evaporated or leaked to the basement

183
00:13:52,161 --> 00:13:56,500
but the leak had at least allowed some of the heat in the reactor to escape

184
00:13:56,501 --> 00:14:03,400
now some parts of the giant furnace are reaching 4,000 degrees but the operators are unaware

185
00:14:03,401 --> 00:14:08,060
temperature monitoring instruments in the core can only read up to 700 degrees

186
00:14:08,061 --> 00:14:12,780
the men sitting in the control room are now flying blind

187
00:14:12,781 --> 00:14:21,060
flying blind is really a scary deeply unnerving experience and it gets worse

188
00:14:21,061 --> 00:14:28,560
bended by minute it's the feeling a person has that no one has ever been in this place before

189
00:14:28,561 --> 00:14:35,280
you're on your own that was the feeling that morning at three mile island experts at the

190
00:14:35,281 --> 00:14:40,860
nuclear regulatory commission in washington and babcock and wilcox the designers of the reactor

191
00:14:40,861 --> 00:14:43,640
desperately try to get through to the control room.

192
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:47,500
But the single phone line in the room is constantly tied up.

193
00:14:47,860 --> 00:14:49,720
The experts get only busy signals.

194
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,560
As if the operators weren't already under enough pressure,

195
00:14:53,900 --> 00:14:55,060
a new alarm sounds.

196
00:14:56,660 --> 00:14:59,600
Radiation is now detected not only in the buildings,

197
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:01,500
but in the control room as well.

198
00:15:03,840 --> 00:15:07,220
Step into what that control room must have been like.

199
00:15:07,700 --> 00:15:09,060
That one phone is ringing.

200
00:15:09,880 --> 00:15:11,820
It's a constant glare.

201
00:15:11,980 --> 00:15:14,240
The master klaxon is going.

202
00:15:14,800 --> 00:15:16,480
The sounds of trouble.

203
00:15:16,920 --> 00:15:21,740
They are supposed to sit down and quietly work out these clues.

204
00:15:22,140 --> 00:15:23,860
It was just almost impossible.

205
00:15:26,700 --> 00:15:28,740
Radiation is measured in REMS.

206
00:15:28,741 --> 00:15:33,220
The maximum radiation a person should receive in a year is five REMS.

207
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:37,660
Readings in the reactor containment building are now reaching 10,000 REMS.

208
00:15:37,661 --> 00:15:44,420
It is only a matter of time until this radiation leaks into the outside atmosphere.

209
00:15:44,421 --> 00:15:48,620
Gary Miller, the station manager for Three Mile Island, now declares a general state

210
00:15:48,621 --> 00:15:55,980
of emergency, the first ever in a nuclear power plant in the United States.

211
00:15:55,981 --> 00:16:01,060
Metropolitan Edison has been slow to release information about what is happening, but at

212
00:16:01,061 --> 00:16:04,800
8.33, the story breaks.

213
00:16:04,801 --> 00:16:09,297
station WKBO in Harrisburg is the first to tell the world.

214
00:16:09,298 --> 00:16:11,700
There is a general emergency at

215
00:16:11,701 --> 00:16:15,766
Metropolitan Edison Company's Three Mile Island nuclear power plant.

216
00:16:15,767 --> 00:16:17,380
A utility sportsman says...

217
00:16:17,381 --> 00:16:21,260
Word of the crisis is also finally reaching Pennsylvania Governor Dick Thornburg.

218
00:16:22,600 --> 00:16:26,160
His Lieutenant Governor, William Scranton, soon appears before the press.

219
00:16:27,140 --> 00:16:31,860
The Metropolitan Edison Company has informed us that there has been an incident at Three Mile

220
00:16:31,861 --> 00:16:36,552
Island unit number two. Everything is under control.

221
00:16:36,553 --> 00:16:39,800
There is and was no danger to public

222
00:16:39,801 --> 00:16:42,630
health and safety. 1017 a.m.

223
00:16:42,631 --> 00:16:49,200
Radiation levels at the top of the containment building could now

224
00:16:49,201 --> 00:16:50,788
kill a man in 20 seconds.

225
00:16:50,789 --> 00:16:55,360
Plant manager Miller now orders his men to break out emergency breathing

226
00:16:55,361 --> 00:16:56,118
apparatus.

227
00:16:56,119 --> 00:17:02,180
By 11 a.m., radiation is detected leaking into the atmosphere outside the plant.

228
00:17:02,940 --> 00:17:05,000
The evil genie is out of the bottle.

229
00:17:08,340 --> 00:17:13,300
By early afternoon, the press is all over the unfolding story of what is happening at

230
00:17:13,301 --> 00:17:18,669
Three Mile Island. Met Ed officials at last call a press conference of their own.

231
00:17:18,670 --> 00:17:19,370
Their

232
00:17:19,371 --> 00:17:24,180
spokesman is Jack Herbine, an engineer with no prior experience in dealing with the press.

233
00:17:25,360 --> 00:17:28,540
The incident occurred this morning around 4 o'clock.

234
00:17:28,680 --> 00:17:30,120
Things are falling off right now.

235
00:17:30,280 --> 00:17:33,160
We expect soon to be in the cold shutdown condition.

236
00:17:34,340 --> 00:17:37,400
Purbine says nothing about the escape of radioactivity

237
00:17:37,401 --> 00:17:40,660
into the air being breathed at that moment by the reporters.

238
00:17:41,120 --> 00:17:44,160
They are left with the impression that the incident is over.

239
00:17:45,180 --> 00:17:46,240
Basically, they just lied.

240
00:17:46,580 --> 00:17:48,700
They didn't call anyone, actually, for hours.

241
00:17:48,701 --> 00:17:51,220
And then they kept insisting that it was just a stuck valve

242
00:17:51,221 --> 00:17:52,980
and they would fix it and there was no real danger.

243
00:17:52,981 --> 00:17:57,980
MedEd's first pronouncements were extremely deceptive and disingenuous.

244
00:17:57,981 --> 00:18:02,980
In fact, it got to the point where the lieutenant governor actually had to give a press conference saying,

245
00:18:02,981 --> 00:18:07,980
look, the information the company is giving us is misleading and non-credible.

246
00:18:07,981 --> 00:18:12,980
MedEd was clearly failing in their efforts to contain the story.

247
00:18:12,981 --> 00:18:17,980
I don't know why we need to tell you each and everything that we do.

248
00:18:17,981 --> 00:18:18,681
We do.

249
00:18:18,682 --> 00:18:21,320
They felt that they knew how to operate this.

250
00:18:21,440 --> 00:18:24,100
They knew how to keep it under control.

251
00:18:24,101 --> 00:18:28,440
And there was a reluctance to admit that things were spiraling out of control.

252
00:18:30,760 --> 00:18:32,440
7.33 p.m.

253
00:18:33,660 --> 00:18:39,100
The reactor designers at Babcock and Wilcox at last get a phone call through to the control room.

254
00:18:40,760 --> 00:18:42,460
The message is urgent and simple.

255
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:44,820
Without water, the reactor will melt.

256
00:18:45,420 --> 00:18:46,760
Forget about going solid.

257
00:18:46,761 --> 00:18:49,260
get water moving through the core.

258
00:18:50,240 --> 00:18:52,300
The pumps are finally restarted,

259
00:18:52,600 --> 00:18:54,440
and for the first time in 15 hours,

260
00:18:54,441 --> 00:18:58,240
the temperature and pressure in the reactor are at last stabilized.

261
00:18:58,960 --> 00:19:00,600
There will be no China syndrome.

262
00:19:01,900 --> 00:19:06,040
But the terrifying drama of this mighty machine is not yet over.

263
00:19:06,400 --> 00:19:08,000
The clock is still ticking.

264
00:19:10,780 --> 00:19:14,400
In 2000, the U.S. Nuclear Regulatory Commission

265
00:19:14,401 --> 00:19:17,360
began renewing permits for American nuclear reactors.

266
00:19:18,020 --> 00:19:21,160
The original permits were good for only 40 years of operation.

267
00:19:22,700 --> 00:19:25,660
Inviting Disaster will continue in a moment on the History Channel.

268
00:19:27,140 --> 00:19:31,340
We now return to Inviting Disaster on Modern Marvels.

269
00:19:32,200 --> 00:19:36,180
In most cases, the mistakes made are ordinary ones.

270
00:19:37,020 --> 00:19:41,280
They are garden variety, the kind of mistakes that you have made, I have made.

271
00:19:41,281 --> 00:19:48,280
What is different about the machine frontier is that the consequences are extraordinary.

272
00:19:50,280 --> 00:19:53,280
Thursday, March 29th, 8.30 a.m.

273
00:19:55,280 --> 00:19:59,280
It is now 28 hours since the accident at Three Mile Island began.

274
00:19:59,281 --> 00:20:03,280
The men in the control room have no way of looking into the reactor,

275
00:20:03,281 --> 00:20:10,280
but it now seems clear that some of the 36,000 slender tubes holding the uranium fuel have cracked.

276
00:20:11,280 --> 00:20:14,940
This is allowing radioactivity to escape into the reactor coolant water.

277
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:20,520
It is imperative operators know how much radioactivity is now in the coolant.

278
00:20:21,060 --> 00:20:23,780
Too much, and the nuclear chain reaction could restart.

279
00:20:24,600 --> 00:20:28,020
Someone must enter the containment building to collect a sample.

280
00:20:28,500 --> 00:20:30,220
It is a task no one wants.

281
00:20:30,740 --> 00:20:34,440
But foreman Ed Hauser agrees to risk his life to take the readings.

282
00:20:35,800 --> 00:20:38,740
He goes in and takes his readings with his Geiger counter.

283
00:20:39,460 --> 00:20:44,220
Now, they say that an X-ray is about one seventy-second thousandth of a rim.

284
00:20:44,940 --> 00:20:49,960
The med-ed people had originally said that the amount of radiation that was being leaked

285
00:20:49,961 --> 00:20:51,560
was no more than you would get in an X-ray.

286
00:20:52,800 --> 00:20:54,900
When Hauser went in, he took his readings,

287
00:20:55,220 --> 00:20:59,460
he found that there were a thousand rims per hour

288
00:20:59,461 --> 00:21:03,340
bouncing off the walls inside the reactor at three mile hour.

289
00:21:04,820 --> 00:21:08,380
He is in for an even greater scare when he draws the coolant sample.

290
00:21:08,740 --> 00:21:11,200
The water from the reactor should be clear.

291
00:21:11,980 --> 00:21:18,520
Instead, he stares at a yellowish, fizzing liquid that's giving off 1,250 rems of radiation.

292
00:21:19,180 --> 00:21:21,260
It actually vibrates in his hand.

293
00:21:22,160 --> 00:21:27,540
If he were foolish enough to continue holding it, the toxic beaker would kill him in less than half an hour.

294
00:21:28,640 --> 00:21:30,560
They knew they had a major problem.

295
00:21:32,740 --> 00:21:38,500
One thing is now clear to the operators at Three Mile Island and the advisors at the Nuclear Regulatory Commission.

296
00:21:38,740 --> 00:21:43,600
The reactor has sustained massive damage and still threatens the plant and the community.

297
00:21:45,060 --> 00:21:47,560
The public is not given these details.

298
00:21:48,180 --> 00:21:50,380
In fact, they have been given little information.

299
00:21:50,620 --> 00:21:53,540
But it is becoming clear to anyone living near Three Mile Island

300
00:21:53,541 --> 00:21:58,340
that something is very wrong with the nuclear plant they have been told was so safe.

301
00:22:00,320 --> 00:22:05,880
The worst thing about Three Mile Island is that people were running from something they couldn't see

302
00:22:05,881 --> 00:22:08,740
or feel or touch or hear.

303
00:22:09,840 --> 00:22:13,460
It's just this haunting, scary feeling

304
00:22:13,461 --> 00:22:15,720
of not knowing what you were exposed to.

305
00:22:17,360 --> 00:22:19,420
Pennsylvania Governor Richard Thornburg

306
00:22:19,421 --> 00:22:22,360
is given an optimistic assessment by MetEd officials,

307
00:22:22,860 --> 00:22:26,100
and based on their assurances, he calls a press conference.

308
00:22:27,940 --> 00:22:32,020
I believe at this point that there is no cause for alarm

309
00:22:32,021 --> 00:22:35,300
or any reason to disrupt your daily routine.

310
00:22:35,301 --> 00:22:41,720
nor any reason to feel that public health has been affected by the events on Three Mile Island.

311
00:22:43,200 --> 00:22:47,040
But by evening, the amount of damage to the plant is becoming clearer.

312
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:52,260
The basement is now filled with several hundred thousand gallons of radioactive water.

313
00:22:52,760 --> 00:22:56,800
This water continues to give off radioactive gas that is building up in the building.

314
00:22:57,520 --> 00:23:00,900
Operators realize that the gas cannot remain inside the plant.

315
00:23:00,901 --> 00:23:04,800
The order is given to vent the dangerous gas into the atmosphere.

316
00:23:05,300 --> 00:23:10,300
Governor Thornburg looks to officials at MedEd and the NRC for advice.

317
00:23:10,301 --> 00:23:15,300
But even the commissioner of the NRC in Washington, Joseph Hendry,

318
00:23:15,301 --> 00:23:20,300
struggles to feel in command of the conflicting information coming at him.

319
00:23:20,301 --> 00:23:26,300
There's a wonderful quote from Joseph Hendry to the then-Governor Thornburg

320
00:23:26,301 --> 00:23:29,300
that he felt, based on the information he was getting,

321
00:23:29,301 --> 00:23:33,300
that they were like two blind men making decisions in the dark.

322
00:23:33,301 --> 00:23:37,600
I mean, this is an incredibly disturbing phenomenon.

323
00:23:37,840 --> 00:23:42,840
People are making decisions about an extremely dangerous technological disaster

324
00:23:42,841 --> 00:23:47,300
based on misinformation, no information, or a lack of information.

325
00:23:48,480 --> 00:23:54,540
Long into the night, Thornburg and his staff continue to wrestle with the decision of whether to evacuate.

326
00:23:55,120 --> 00:24:00,580
A half million Pennsylvanians live within a strong breeze of the now dangerous nuclear plant.

327
00:24:00,581 --> 00:24:02,820
Their lives could hang in the balance.

328
00:24:03,300 --> 00:24:10,360
He wrestled with this because, as the record shows, his concern was that bad things happen

329
00:24:10,361 --> 00:24:12,160
during a chaotic evacuation.

330
00:24:12,161 --> 00:24:18,320
You know, people get killed in accidents, you know, looting may go on, you know, that

331
00:24:18,321 --> 00:24:19,021
sort of thing.

332
00:24:21,200 --> 00:24:25,900
Thornburg knows a full evacuation will not be as easy as just reading an announcement.

333
00:24:26,820 --> 00:24:27,920
Roads will be jammed.

334
00:24:28,540 --> 00:24:29,600
And what about the disabled?

335
00:24:29,601 --> 00:24:35,160
hospital patients, the elderly, and people without a car. Nothing in his political

336
00:24:35,161 --> 00:24:40,000
career has prepared Thornburg for this situation.

337
00:24:42,480 --> 00:24:49,760
Friday 8 a.m. In a suburb of Washington DC, members of the Nuclear Regulatory

338
00:24:49,761 --> 00:24:54,840
Commission's incident response team meet with the director of regulation Harold

339
00:24:54,841 --> 00:25:01,240
Denton. Denton is told that radiation levels inside Three Mile Island now

340
00:25:01,241 --> 00:25:07,080
require that they recommend preliminary evacuation to the governor.

341
00:25:07,081 --> 00:25:09,267
Could I hear attention please?

342
00:25:09,268 --> 00:25:14,080
There has been a state of emergency declared on Three Mile Island.

343
00:25:14,081 --> 00:25:19,920
Already local authorities are warning the public, but finally by late morning

344
00:25:19,921 --> 00:25:23,020
Governor Thornburg becomes even more cautious.

345
00:25:23,500 --> 00:25:26,640
Based on advice of the chairman of the NRC,

346
00:25:27,580 --> 00:25:31,980
I am advising pregnant women and preschool-aged children

347
00:25:31,981 --> 00:25:36,280
to leave the area within a five-mile radius

348
00:25:36,281 --> 00:25:40,380
of the Three Mile Island facility until further notice.

349
00:25:40,700 --> 00:25:44,540
My sister was in school, and parents just came

350
00:25:44,541 --> 00:25:46,820
and took their children out of school.

351
00:25:46,821 --> 00:25:49,680
There was that sense of panic, that sense of fear.

352
00:25:49,920 --> 00:25:50,780
I'm through that!

353
00:25:53,260 --> 00:25:58,040
Although Thornburg's evacuation order applies to only some 40,000 residents,

354
00:25:58,680 --> 00:26:01,780
over 100,000 people now take to the road.

355
00:26:05,120 --> 00:26:10,400
New York Times reporter Wilborn Hampton remembers driving through the small town of Goldsboro

356
00:26:10,401 --> 00:26:12,820
just across the river from the stricken plant.

357
00:26:13,320 --> 00:26:15,180
It was like driving into a ghost town.

358
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:17,860
The only thing that was missing were the tumbleweeds.

359
00:26:17,861 --> 00:26:20,360
wasn't a car on the street

360
00:26:20,361 --> 00:26:22,460
drive by the post office, it's locked

361
00:26:22,461 --> 00:26:26,200
front doors standing open

362
00:26:26,201 --> 00:26:30,260
one house TV was still on

363
00:26:30,261 --> 00:26:32,640
people had just gotten up and left

364
00:26:32,641 --> 00:26:34,460
as fast as they could

365
00:26:34,461 --> 00:26:36,080
the fear that existed

366
00:26:36,081 --> 00:26:38,300
in the towns around

367
00:26:38,301 --> 00:26:39,260
Three Mile Island

368
00:26:39,261 --> 00:26:42,180
was very hard to describe, I'd never seen anything

369
00:26:42,181 --> 00:26:43,960
quite like it and I had covered

370
00:26:43,961 --> 00:26:44,940
two or three wars

371
00:26:44,941 --> 00:26:46,800
but it was

372
00:26:46,801 --> 00:26:49,360
It was the fear of the unknown.

373
00:26:51,540 --> 00:26:53,120
Towns are being evacuated.

374
00:26:53,640 --> 00:26:56,220
Radioactive gases drift from the damaged reactor,

375
00:26:56,740 --> 00:26:58,840
and core temperatures are still off the chart.

376
00:26:59,380 --> 00:27:02,500
Radioactivity in the reactor containment building is so intense,

377
00:27:02,780 --> 00:27:06,380
insulation is burning off wiring, causing instruments to fail.

378
00:27:06,900 --> 00:27:09,980
Little by little, less and less will be known about the wounded,

379
00:27:10,200 --> 00:27:12,060
but still very dangerous reactor.

380
00:27:12,061 --> 00:27:19,060
Now, an NRC engineer announces an even greater terror may be waiting to strike.

381
00:27:19,061 --> 00:27:27,060
By analyzing new computer data, Roger Mattson is convinced that melting fuel rods have released a large amount of hydrogen

382
00:27:27,061 --> 00:27:30,060
that has now gathered at the top of the containment dome.

383
00:27:30,061 --> 00:27:34,060
With the right amount of oxygen, this hydrogen bubble can explode,

384
00:27:34,061 --> 00:27:41,060
instantly blowing the top off the building and filling the air for hundreds of miles with deadly radioactive gas.

385
00:27:41,061 --> 00:27:45,090
gas. The world has never known a day quite like today.

386
00:27:45,091 --> 00:27:48,000
It faced the considerable uncertainties

387
00:27:48,001 --> 00:27:52,420
and dangers of the worst nuclear power plant accident of the atomic age.

388
00:27:52,421 --> 00:27:53,280
And the horror

389
00:27:53,281 --> 00:28:00,040
tonight is that it could get much worse. Reporters will come to call this day Black

390
00:28:00,041 --> 00:28:00,741
Friday.

391
00:28:00,742 --> 00:28:05,760
All who are near the stricken plant hold their breath to see what the next day

392
00:28:05,761 --> 00:28:12,043
will bring. We now return to inviting disaster on modern marvels.

393
00:28:12,044 --> 00:28:15,040
One thing we can be sure of now

394
00:28:15,041 --> 00:28:19,011
is that the public's trust can be lost rather quickly.

395
00:28:19,012 --> 00:28:22,100
One mistake, one irreversible consequence,

396
00:28:22,920 --> 00:28:28,620
the trust goes away and those people on the machine frontier might lose that spot forever.

397
00:28:28,621 --> 00:28:33,080
Saturday, 10 a.m.

398
00:28:33,620 --> 00:28:36,520
Reporters at Three Mile Island are now asking questions

399
00:28:36,521 --> 00:28:37,980
not to get a news story,

400
00:28:38,480 --> 00:28:41,140
but to have a sense of whether they should be running for their lives.

401
00:28:41,800 --> 00:28:44,180
From almost the moment the story reached them,

402
00:28:44,440 --> 00:28:47,480
the press has felt there was no one in authority they could trust.

403
00:28:48,040 --> 00:28:50,520
Met Ed officials obviously are spinning the facts,

404
00:28:50,880 --> 00:28:53,760
and the NRC reactions are confused and contradictory.

405
00:28:55,380 --> 00:28:57,860
Among the people most concerned by the confusion

406
00:28:57,861 --> 00:29:00,140
is the President of the United States.

407
00:29:00,720 --> 00:29:02,420
Jimmy Carter is a trained engineer

408
00:29:02,421 --> 00:29:05,940
and a veteran of the Navy's illustrious nuclear submarine program.

409
00:29:06,400 --> 00:29:08,800
He now tells NRC Commissioner Joseph Hendry

410
00:29:08,801 --> 00:29:11,920
that he wants a personal representative at Three Mile Island

411
00:29:11,921 --> 00:29:15,160
to take charge of activities and report directly to him.

412
00:29:16,040 --> 00:29:17,760
Hendry turns to Harold Denton.

413
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:21,920
Denton is an engineer who has been leading the emergency team

414
00:29:21,921 --> 00:29:24,180
at NRC headquarters in Bethesda, Maryland.

415
00:29:24,181 --> 00:29:27,340
Now he arrives in Pennsylvania with presidential authority.

416
00:29:27,860 --> 00:29:30,640
Almost immediately, the mood begins to change.

417
00:29:31,660 --> 00:29:39,160
Harold Denton of the NRC was sent in to be kind of like the reassuring public figure

418
00:29:39,161 --> 00:29:44,660
who could give out the information and have people actually believe him.

419
00:29:44,661 --> 00:29:49,220
When Harold Denton arrived, there suddenly was a sense that here was someone you could trust.

420
00:29:49,740 --> 00:29:55,700
And it was a great relief after days of talking to publicists and press people from Metropolitan Medicine.

421
00:29:55,701 --> 00:29:59,800
I am advising those who may be particularly susceptible.

422
00:29:59,801 --> 00:30:03,300
Thornburg had ordered the evacuation of pregnant women and children.

423
00:30:03,301 --> 00:30:08,020
Now, if Roger Mattson's warning about the danger of the hydrogen bubble is correct,

424
00:30:08,021 --> 00:30:11,940
every resident in a 10-mile radius should be evacuated.

425
00:30:11,941 --> 00:30:15,220
The problems of such a task would be extraordinary,

426
00:30:15,221 --> 00:30:19,520
but so would the loss of life if the governor makes a mistake.

427
00:30:19,521 --> 00:30:23,400
Governor Thornburg was faced with a difficult situation.

428
00:30:23,401 --> 00:30:25,960
I wouldn't have wanted to be in his shoes for the world.

429
00:30:27,800 --> 00:30:30,240
Harold Denton has looked carefully at the data

430
00:30:30,241 --> 00:30:33,080
and talked to other engineers about the hydrogen bubble

431
00:30:33,081 --> 00:30:34,960
and the immediate danger of meltdown.

432
00:30:35,440 --> 00:30:38,200
He is more confident about the situation than Roger Mattson.

433
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:41,740
Governor Thornburg decides to gamble on Denton's opinion.

434
00:30:42,340 --> 00:30:47,340
But it's certainly days before flammability limits would be reached

435
00:30:47,341 --> 00:30:51,200
and many more days after that before detonation limits would be reached,

436
00:30:51,201 --> 00:30:57,000
all of which assume that we did nothing but sit on our hands here instead of getting the hydrogen out of the vessel.

437
00:30:58,400 --> 00:31:01,980
At the same press conference, the governor makes a shocking announcement,

438
00:31:02,460 --> 00:31:05,700
one that will do much to at last end many people's fears.

439
00:31:06,340 --> 00:31:16,240
President Carter will be paying a visit to the area to make a personal on-site visit.

440
00:31:16,241 --> 00:31:26,861
But what most of America does not understand is that there is still strong disagreement within the NRC as to whether the hydrogen bubble is close to blowing the roof

441
00:31:26,862 --> 00:31:30,980
off the containment building, perhaps with the president inside.

442
00:31:31,760 --> 00:31:34,185
The president did take a chance by coming.

443
00:31:34,186 --> 00:31:42,960
He had advice from some of his people that he really shouldn't go, that it was still not safe enough for the president of the United States to go there.

444
00:31:42,961 --> 00:31:45,132
But he was a nuclear engineer.

445
00:31:45,133 --> 00:31:53,460
He knew probably better than any other public elected official, you know, what was going on, what the dangers were.

446
00:31:57,280 --> 00:31:59,380
Sunday, April 1st, 10 a.m.

447
00:32:01,760 --> 00:32:05,460
Even as the president's helicopter comes in for a landing in Harrisburg,

448
00:32:05,760 --> 00:32:12,720
Matson and Victor Stello, another NRC engineer, are locked in a heated dispute over the threat of the hydrogen bubble.

449
00:32:12,960 --> 00:32:18,960
Harold Denton briefs the president and Mrs. Carter on the situation.

450
00:32:18,961 --> 00:32:24,960
He is very direct and tells the president of the dispute between his staff as to the present danger at the plant.

451
00:32:24,961 --> 00:32:29,960
Carter doesn't hesitate. He starts downriver toward Three Mile Island.

452
00:32:29,961 --> 00:32:31,778
The president tours the plant.

453
00:32:31,779 --> 00:32:37,960
The tour does not uncover any new facts for the president's consideration or the public's consumption.

454
00:32:37,961 --> 00:32:41,960
Its purpose is simple, restore public confidence.

455
00:32:41,961 --> 00:32:46,420
When the president came along with his wife,

456
00:32:46,580 --> 00:32:49,080
we felt that the president of the United States

457
00:32:49,081 --> 00:32:51,240
is not going to come if it's not safe.

458
00:32:51,700 --> 00:32:55,200
And when he came, it really was a relief

459
00:32:55,201 --> 00:32:56,640
as far as the people were concerned.

460
00:32:58,240 --> 00:33:00,760
But the important moment of Carter's visit

461
00:33:00,761 --> 00:33:03,780
is not taking place with the president inside the plant,

462
00:33:03,781 --> 00:33:07,480
but in an office trailer where Roger Mattson and Victor Stella

463
00:33:07,481 --> 00:33:10,520
frantically analyze chemical calculations together.

464
00:33:11,960 --> 00:33:18,760
As the president's visit is concluding, Stella at last finds the error in the calculations that proves his point.

465
00:33:19,060 --> 00:33:21,980
They do have time, days, perhaps weeks.

466
00:33:22,520 --> 00:33:26,960
The hydrogen can be released gradually, ending the danger of a massive explosion.

467
00:33:27,480 --> 00:33:31,520
And finally, engineers will be able to slowly shut down the reactor.

468
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:33,900
The end of the crisis is in sight.

469
00:33:36,140 --> 00:33:38,360
Disaster was averted at Three Mile Island.

470
00:33:38,361 --> 00:33:43,100
but the scale of what might have been is difficult for most people to imagine.

471
00:33:44,620 --> 00:33:48,460
Yes, there was talk of radioactivity and some sort of meltdown,

472
00:33:48,780 --> 00:33:53,440
and a few thousand people were temporarily forced from their homes, but no one died.

473
00:33:54,300 --> 00:33:58,440
To anyone living in another part of America, not much seems to have happened.

474
00:34:00,340 --> 00:34:05,360
To begin to comprehend what almost happened on a small island in the Susquehanna River,

475
00:34:05,361 --> 00:34:10,400
it is necessary to look at another nuclear power plant half a world away.

476
00:34:12,740 --> 00:34:16,100
Before nuclear power, uranium had little value,

477
00:34:16,580 --> 00:34:19,540
and then only as a coloring agent for porcelain and glass.

478
00:34:20,860 --> 00:34:23,820
Inviting Disaster will continue in a moment on the History Channel.

479
00:34:26,420 --> 00:34:30,620
We now return to Inviting Disaster on Modern Marvels.

480
00:34:30,621 --> 00:34:43,065
The history of machines tells us that the most likely time for a problem, for error, for a malfunction, is when a big, complex machine is moving from one state to

481
00:34:43,066 --> 00:34:43,766
another.

482
00:34:43,767 --> 00:34:49,857
It is changing its speed. It is being started up. It is being shut down.

483
00:34:49,858 --> 00:34:52,774
It is an airplane that is landing.

484
00:34:52,775 --> 00:34:59,380
Those are changes of state that need a higher than normal level of alertness.

485
00:35:00,620 --> 00:35:09,620
Like Three Mile Island, the Chernobyl nuclear plant sits on a river back.

486
00:35:09,621 --> 00:35:15,620
This one is the Pripyat River, 80 miles north of the Ukrainian capital of Kyiv.

487
00:35:15,621 --> 00:35:20,620
Seven years earlier, catastrophe had been narrowly averted at Three Mile Island.

488
00:35:20,621 --> 00:35:28,620
But on a spring day in 1986, the farm-filled countryside around this huge plant will not be so lucky.

489
00:35:30,620 --> 00:35:34,500
Unit 4 at Chernobyl had been scheduled for a maintenance shutdown.

490
00:35:36,220 --> 00:35:40,560
Even routine maintenance moves a complex machine from one state to another.

491
00:35:41,560 --> 00:35:43,100
It is a time of great danger.

492
00:35:43,880 --> 00:35:46,380
Normal procedure is interrupted or set aside.

493
00:35:46,980 --> 00:35:48,580
Safety checklists are changed.

494
00:35:49,220 --> 00:35:52,820
But on this day, authorities have changed the status quo even more.

495
00:35:53,480 --> 00:35:58,720
They have ordered that a test be conducted to see how much electricity can be pulled from the generators

496
00:35:58,721 --> 00:36:02,500
while the reactor is losing power and slowing to a halt.

497
00:36:03,580 --> 00:36:07,600
The thing was hard to keep going at low power.

498
00:36:08,600 --> 00:36:15,200
And one by one, they had to disable the various automatic systems

499
00:36:15,201 --> 00:36:17,220
that would have shut down the reactor.

500
00:36:18,620 --> 00:36:25,720
This big, grumpy beast did not like to keep walking at this awkward speed.

501
00:36:25,721 --> 00:36:28,380
In fact, the reactor fought back.

502
00:36:28,720 --> 00:36:34,480
tried to shut itself down completely as it approached that dangerous low power state.

503
00:36:35,360 --> 00:36:42,240
The operators took steps to keep it running at a lower power, pushing that edge because they had

504
00:36:42,241 --> 00:36:46,880
been told, work out this experiment, see how much power you can squeeze.

505
00:36:48,800 --> 00:36:52,300
Now the reactor is surging into unknown territory.

506
00:36:52,301 --> 00:36:55,520
Within minutes, it is clear to plant operators

507
00:36:55,521 --> 00:36:57,580
that the machine is running away with itself.

508
00:36:58,100 --> 00:36:59,460
They decide to shut it down.

509
00:37:01,060 --> 00:37:05,480
And they threw the switch to drive in the control rods,

510
00:37:05,580 --> 00:37:07,300
which were also the shutdown rods.

511
00:37:07,680 --> 00:37:11,220
And unfortunately, the design of those rods

512
00:37:11,221 --> 00:37:14,860
had the effect of making the situation worse

513
00:37:14,861 --> 00:37:15,680
and set it better.

514
00:37:19,340 --> 00:37:22,280
With a force of 45 tons of dynamite,

515
00:37:22,281 --> 00:37:26,640
The reactor explodes and rips off the top of the containment building.

516
00:37:27,240 --> 00:37:29,580
Tons of steel are thrown in all directions.

517
00:37:30,500 --> 00:37:36,320
But what was also blown apart was one of the largest collections of cancer-causing,

518
00:37:36,560 --> 00:37:40,680
death-causing poison that humans had ever assembled in one place.

519
00:37:41,440 --> 00:37:45,740
As helicopters and plant workers pour tons of water onto the raging inferno,

520
00:37:46,180 --> 00:37:49,420
huge clouds of radioactive steam pour into the air.

521
00:37:50,280 --> 00:37:53,960
They begin a terrifying migration across all of northern Europe.

522
00:37:54,640 --> 00:37:59,460
In what amounts to a suicide mission, workers frantically try to bring the fire under control.

523
00:38:02,080 --> 00:38:08,380
While Soviet media try to put the best face on the disaster, the awful results soon become apparent.

524
00:38:10,220 --> 00:38:16,040
Over 2,000 workers die in the initial explosion and the intense radiation that follows.

525
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:20,640
Another 6,000 will die when they are ordered to build a containment shell

526
00:38:20,641 --> 00:38:23,620
over the ruined but still very deadly reactor.

527
00:38:25,380 --> 00:38:31,340
I have a great deal of respect to those people who were going inside the shelter.

528
00:38:31,341 --> 00:38:38,280
They knew that there was a 90% probability that they would die of radiation eventually.

529
00:38:39,040 --> 00:38:40,340
And yet...

530
00:38:40,341 --> 00:38:44,340
I'm sorry, I can't...

531
00:38:44,341 --> 00:38:47,240
I am sure that most of them died.

532
00:38:50,540 --> 00:38:54,980
The nearby city of Pripyat, once home to 50,000 people,

533
00:38:55,560 --> 00:38:58,840
became a ghost town as its residents were evacuated from the region.

534
00:39:00,340 --> 00:39:03,840
In the years since, cancer rates and birth defects

535
00:39:03,841 --> 00:39:07,300
throughout the Ukraine and neighboring Belarus have skyrocketed.

536
00:39:08,200 --> 00:39:11,460
Today, the containment shell that was poorly built in haste

537
00:39:11,461 --> 00:39:13,420
around the still radioactive plant

538
00:39:13,421 --> 00:39:14,980
threatens to collapse.

539
00:39:15,580 --> 00:39:19,680
If it does, it will again spill its poison into the environment.

540
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:23,640
Some of the statistics are really frightening.

541
00:39:24,620 --> 00:39:28,960
No one will ever farm within probably a hundred mile radius of that place

542
00:39:28,961 --> 00:39:31,140
for decades, perhaps a hundred years.

543
00:39:31,620 --> 00:39:35,200
If you have to doubt that attention must be paid to these things,

544
00:39:35,280 --> 00:39:38,920
just look at some of the incidents and statistics from Chernobyl.

545
00:39:39,720 --> 00:39:43,000
So it gives a glimpse, just a glimpse, a horrific glimpse,

546
00:39:43,420 --> 00:39:46,660
of what could have happened in Pennsylvania.

547
00:39:49,620 --> 00:39:53,000
Three Mile Island was spared the horror of Chernobyl.

548
00:39:53,800 --> 00:39:57,140
The great cooling towers of Unit 2 still rise to the sky.

549
00:39:58,020 --> 00:40:00,520
But the unit has never again produced electricity.

550
00:40:02,920 --> 00:40:06,260
Radiation levels in some parts of its buildings remain so high

551
00:40:06,261 --> 00:40:08,800
that no one has dared go there since the accident.

552
00:40:08,801 --> 00:40:17,980
In July of 1983, technicians at last were able to lower video cameras into the core of the reactor.

553
00:40:18,640 --> 00:40:21,540
What they found confirmed what had only been feared.

554
00:40:22,220 --> 00:40:26,720
Over 50% of the reactor's core had vanished on that day in 1979,

555
00:40:27,380 --> 00:40:30,580
turning over 20 tons of uranium into molten sludge.

556
00:40:30,581 --> 00:40:39,000
30 to 60 minutes is all that separated Three Mile Island from the unspeakable

557
00:40:39,001 --> 00:40:41,600
Had complete reactor meltdown occurred

558
00:40:41,601 --> 00:40:45,020
As many as 3,000 people might have died immediately

559
00:40:45,021 --> 00:40:49,340
Another 45,000 could have died from radiation cancer

560
00:40:49,341 --> 00:40:54,220
Harrisburg, Lancaster, even the Gettysburg National Battlefield Park

561
00:40:54,221 --> 00:40:57,500
All could have become uninhabitable for decades

562
00:40:57,501 --> 00:41:02,620
But fortunately, no one died at Three Mile Island

563
00:41:02,621 --> 00:41:05,640
Ultimately, the physical plant did its job

564
00:41:05,641 --> 00:41:07,880
Most radiation was contained

565
00:41:07,881 --> 00:41:10,100
The meltdown was only partial

566
00:41:10,101 --> 00:41:15,120
Although Unit 2 remains locked and impossible to enter for more than a few minutes

567
00:41:15,121 --> 00:41:19,320
Its companion, Unit 1, continues to produce electricity

568
00:41:19,321 --> 00:41:24,060
But five frightening days in the spring of 1979

569
00:41:24,061 --> 00:41:27,820
changed forever the face of nuclear power in America.

570
00:41:29,000 --> 00:41:33,380
They ended the nation's blind infatuation with the atomic miracle.

571
00:41:34,580 --> 00:41:38,740
Since the accident, no new reactor licenses have been applied for.

572
00:41:40,280 --> 00:41:42,060
Because of Three Mile Island,

573
00:41:42,061 --> 00:41:45,680
nuclear plants today are safer, better equipped,

574
00:41:46,080 --> 00:41:49,740
and are operated by personnel better trained to handle emergencies.

575
00:41:51,140 --> 00:41:54,000
Carefully designed evacuation and contingency plans

576
00:41:54,001 --> 00:41:54,906
are in place.

577
00:41:54,907 --> 00:42:00,480
Most important, no one involved in nuclear power will ever again tell the public

578
00:42:00,481 --> 00:42:01,905
it can't happen here.

579
00:42:01,906 --> 00:42:06,860
Some of the most dramatic changes have been in how the Nuclear Regulatory

580
00:42:06,861 --> 00:42:10,597
Commission is prepared to deal with any future accidents.

581
00:42:10,598 --> 00:42:12,760
I'd like to show you the facility

582
00:42:12,761 --> 00:42:19,160
that we developed that reflects many of the lessons learned as a result of the Three Mile

583
00:42:19,161 --> 00:42:20,494
Island accident.

584
00:42:20,495 --> 00:42:26,580
Of most importance is a direct line that we have from here to every plant

585
00:42:26,581 --> 00:42:28,229
site in the United States.

586
00:42:28,230 --> 00:42:32,160
It not only ties in voice communication links and allows these

587
00:42:32,161 --> 00:42:37,900
individuals to talk to their counterparts on site, but it also has a line that goes

588
00:42:37,901 --> 00:42:39,658
into the plant computer.

589
00:42:39,659 --> 00:42:44,420
What we do with that information is analyze it, turn it over, and

590
00:42:44,421 --> 00:42:51,140
attempt to characterize what are the most important parts of the safety

591
00:42:51,141 --> 00:42:57,380
systems that we should be concerned with. But we have also learned other less

592
00:42:57,381 --> 00:43:01,400
tangible lessons. They are lessons that have less to do with hardware and

593
00:43:01,401 --> 00:43:06,980
engineering and more to do with human attitudes toward technology.

594
00:43:06,981 --> 00:43:20,380
Complex technologies work best when they're backed up by skepticism and checking, not bravado, not confidence that nothing can go wrong.

595
00:43:20,920 --> 00:43:27,220
Complacency and bravado are recipes for surprises and very nasty surprises.

596
00:43:27,221 --> 00:43:39,600
as our technologies advance and as our power to wipe ourselves out increases it is vital that our

597
00:43:39,601 --> 00:43:48,200
wisdom increase at at least as fast a rate as our knowledge and wisdom and knowledge are not the same

598
00:43:48,201 --> 00:43:56,960
things you know i think it's important that these cooling towers remain here forever

599
00:43:56,961 --> 00:43:59,860
as a monument to a failed technology.

600
00:44:00,220 --> 00:44:02,940
And, you know, like any battlefield that you go to study,

601
00:44:03,020 --> 00:44:05,360
this is a battlefield where technology failed.

602
00:44:05,740 --> 00:44:09,260
And you can't study nuclear power without coming to Three Mile Island.

603
00:44:09,261 --> 00:44:12,580
It would be like studying the Civil War without going to Gettysburg.

604
00:44:15,240 --> 00:44:20,100
Of the 37,000 people who lived within five miles of these giant towers

605
00:44:20,101 --> 00:44:23,820
when the accident happened, only half remain living there.

606
00:44:23,821 --> 00:44:30,820
Those who stayed here express a confidence that society has learned well the lessons of Three Mile Island.

607
00:44:30,821 --> 00:44:40,820
Those who have moved away from this place remind us that we must forever be on guard for the mistakes and arrogance that can bring about disaster.

